\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 A panel is a special kind of window, typically serving an auxiliary function in an application. NSPanel adds a few special behaviors to NSWindow in support of the role panels play:\
\fs14\
\pard \s5 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx6300 \tx6590 \tx15154 \fs28 \'b7\tab Panels are by default not released when they\'27re closed, since they\'27re usually lightweight and often reused.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab On-screen panels, except for attention panels, are removed from the screen when the application isn\'27t active, and are restored when the application again becomes active. This reduces screen clutter.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab Panels can become the key window, but not the main window.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab If a panel is the key window and has a close button, it closes itself when the user presses the Escape key.\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 In addition to these automatic behaviors, NSPanel allows you to configure certain other behaviors common to some kinds of panels:\
\fs14\
\pard \s5 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx6300 \tx6590 \tx15154 \fs28 \'b7\tab A panel can be precluded from becoming the key window unless the user clicks in a view that responds to typing. This prevents key window from shifting to the panel unnecessarily. The \b setBecomesKeyOnlyIfNeeded:\b0  method controls this behavior.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab Palettes and similar panels can be made to float above standard windows and other panels. This prevents them from being covered and keeps them readily available to the user. The \b setFloatingPanel:\b0  method controls this behavior.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab A panel can be made to receive mouse and keyboard events even when another window or panel is being run modally or run in a modal session. This permits actions in the panel to affect the modal window or panel. The \b setWorksWhenModal:\b0  method controls this behavior. See \'aaModal Windows\'ba in the NSWindow class specification for more information on modal windows and panels.\
\fs14\
\fs56\
