\s41 \li1512 \fi0 \fs28 {{\NeXTHelpMarker4812 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_method:[NSPanel-becomesKeyOnlyIfNeeded];}
¬}\pard \s41 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql becomesKeyOnlyIfNeeded\
\fs6\
\s39 \li2520 \fi-604 \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80(BOOL)\b becomesKeyOnlyIfNeeded\
\fs14\
\b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Returns YES if the receiver becomes the key window only when the user clicks a view object that needs to be first responder to receive event and action messages; for example if it edits text or otherwise accepts keyboard input. Returns NO if it becomes the key window whenever clicked. NSPanel by default returns NO, indicating that panels become key as other windows do.\
\fs14\
\pard \s58 \li2804 \fi-1292 \ri1007 \ql \tx2799 \tx3282 \tx3771 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 See also:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80\b setBecomesKeyOnlyIfNeeded:\b0 , \f3 -\f1 \'80\b needsPanelToBecomeKey\b0 \'80(NSView)\
\fs14\
\fs46\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s40 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx8265 {{\NeXTHelpMarker5612 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_method:[NSPanel-isFloatingPanel];}
¬}\pard \s40 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx8265 isFloatingPanel\
\fs6\
\pard \s39 \li2520 \fi-604 \ri1007 \ql \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80(BOOL)\b isFloatingPanel\
\fs14\
\b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Returns YES if the receiver is set to float above normal windows, NO otherwise. A floating panel\'27s window level is NSFloatingWindowLevel. NSPanels by default returns NO, indicating that they inhabit the normal window level.\
\fs14\
\pard \s58 \li2804 \fi-1292 \ri1007 \ql \tx2799 \tx3282 \tx3771 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 See also:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80\b setFloatingPanel:\b0 , \f3 -\f1 \'80\b level\b0 \'80(NSWindow)\
\fs14\
\fs46\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s40 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx8265 {{\NeXTHelpMarker6412 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_method:[NSPanel-setBecomesKeyOnlyIfNeeded:];}
¬}\pard \s40 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx8265 setBecomesKeyOnlyIfNeeded:\
\fs6\
\pard \s39 \li2520 \fi-604 \ri1007 \ql \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80(void)\b setBecomesKeyOnlyIfNeeded:\b0 (BOOL)\i flag\
\fs14\
\i0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Controls whether the receiver only becomes the key window when the user clicks a view object that edits text or otherwise accepts keyboard input. If \i flag\i0  is YES, the receiver only becomes the key window when keyboard input is needed; if \i flag\i0  is NO, it becomes the key window whenever clicked. This behavior is by default not set. You should consider setting it only if most controls in the NSPanel aren\'27t text fields, and if the choices that can be made by entering text can also be made in another way (such as by clicking an item in a pick list).\
\fs14\
\pard \s58 \li2804 \fi-1292 \ri1007 \ql \tx2799 \tx3282 \tx3771 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 See also:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80\b becomesKeyOnlyIfNeeded\b0 , \f3 -\f1 \'80\b needsPanelToBecomeKey\b0 \'80(NSView)\
\fs14\
\fs46\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s40 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx8265 {{\NeXTHelpMarker7213 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_method:[NSPanel-setFloatingPanel:];}
¬}\pard \s40 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx8265 setFloatingPanel:\
\fs6\
\pard \s39 \li2520 \fi-604 \ri1007 \ql \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80(void)\b setFloatingPanel:\b0 (BOOL)\i flag\
\fs14\
\i0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Controls whether the receiver floats above normal windows. If \i flag\i0  is YES, sets the receiver\'27s window level to NSFloatingWindowLevel; if \i flag\i0  is NO, sets the receiver\'27s window level to NSNormalWindowLevel. The default is NO. It\'27s appropriate for an NSPanel to float above other windows only if all of the following conditions are true:\
\fs14\
\pard \s5 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx6300 \tx6590 \tx15154 \fs28 \'b7\tab It\'27s small enough not to obscure whatever\'27s behind it.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab It\'27s oriented more to the mouse than to the keyboard\'d0that is, if it doesn\'27t become the key window or becomes so only when needed.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab It needs to remain visible while the user works in the application\'27s normal windows; for example, if the user must frequently move the cursor back and forth between a normal window and the panel (such as a tool palette), or if the panel gives information relevant to the user\'27s actions in a normal window.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab It hides when the application is deactivated (the default behavior for panels).\
\fs14\
\pard \s58 \li2804 \fi-1292 \ri1007 \ql \tx2799 \tx3282 \tx3771 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 See also:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80\b isFloatingPanel\b0 , \f3 -\f1 \'80\b setWindowLevel:\b0 \'80(NSWindow)\
\fs14\
\fs46\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s40 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx8265 {{\NeXTHelpMarker8812 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_method:[NSPanel-setWorksWhenModal:];}
¬}\pard \s40 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx8265 setWorksWhenModal:\
\fs6\
\pard \s39 \li2520 \fi-604 \ri1007 \ql \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80(void)\b setWorksWhenModal:\b0 (BOOL)\i flag\
\fs14\
\i0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Controls whether the receiver receives keyboard and mouse events even when some other window is being run modally. If \i flag\i0  is YES, the application object sends events to the receiver even during a modal loop or session; if \i flag\i0  is NO, the receiver gets no events while a modal loop or session is running. See \'aaModal Windows\'ba in the NSWindow class specification for more information on modal windows and panels.\
\fs14\
\pard \s58 \li2804 \fi-1292 \ri1007 \ql \tx2799 \tx3282 \tx3771 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 See also:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80\b worksWhenModal\b0 , \f3 -\f1 \'80\b runModalForWindow:\b0 \'80(NSApplication), \f3 -\f1 \'80\b runModalSession:\b0 \'80(NSApplication)\
\fs14\
\fs46\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s40 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx8265 {{\NeXTHelpMarker9612 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_method:[NSPanel-worksWhenModal];}
¬}\pard \s40 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx8265 worksWhenModal\
\fs6\
\pard \s39 \li2520 \fi-604 \ri1007 \ql \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80(BOOL)\b worksWhenModal\
\fs14\
\b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Returns YES if the receiver is able to receive keyboard and mouse events even when some other window is being run modally, NO otherwise. NSPanels by default returns NO, indicating their ineligibility for events during a modal loop or session. See \'aaModal Windows\'ba in the NSWindow class specification for more information on modal windows and panels.\
\fs14\
\pard \s58 \li2804 \fi-1292 \ri1007 \ql \tx2799 \tx3282 \tx3771 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 See also:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80\b setWorksWhenModal:\b0 , \f3 -\f1 \'80\b runModalForWindow:\b0 \'80(NSApplication), \f3 -\f1 \'80\b runModalSession:\b0 \'80(NSApplication)\
\fs14\
